<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Midian</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Madyan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Midian was a region in the Tabuk Province of modern Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, on the east shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>, mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> makes Midian a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and Keturah and says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> spent forty years there, marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipporah">Zipporah</a>, daughter of the priest Jethro. Some scholars think the name first denoted a tribal confederation. Midianite pottery, also called Qurayyah Painted Ware, is found from the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> to north-west Arabia, and a Midianite tent shrine at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-mining site of Timna offers a close parallel to the biblical Tabernacle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/midian.jpg" alt="Midian" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bandar Al-Huwaifi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:مدين.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
